2026-07-30T05:43:55.2040968Z             "body": ".NET 8 reaches end of support on **10 November 2026** — about 15 weeks out. This moves the whole solution to .NET 10, the LTS that replaces it (supported to November 2028), and brings every package up with it.\n\n## What changed\n\n| Area | From | To |\n|---|---|---|\n| Target framework (×5 projects) | `net8.0` | `net10.0` |\n| System.CommandLine | 2.0.0-beta4 | 2.0.10 |\n| Swashbuckle.AspNetCore | 6.6.2 | 10.2.3 |\n| StackExchange.Redis | 2.8.16 | 3.0.17 |\n| Spectre.Console | 0.48.0 | 0.57.2 |\n| JwtBearer / OpenIdConnect | 8.0.29 | 10.0.10 |\n| Caching.Memory · Mvc.Testing | 8.0.1 · 8.0.29 | 10.0.10 |\n| DnsClient | 1.7.0 | 1.8.0 |\n| Test SDK · xunit · vs runner · coverlet | 17.6.0 · 2.4.2 · 2.4.5 · 6.0.0 | 18.8.1 · 2.9.3 · 3.1.5 · 10.0.1 |\n\nTwo package references are **deleted** rather than bumped: `System.Text.Json` (was pinned at 10.0.5 to outrun net8.0's serializer; on net10.0 it is in the shared framework, where an explicit reference can only pin something *older* than the runtime) and `Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Console` (part of `Microsoft.AspNetCore.App`).\n\nAlso updated: `Dockerfile` base images, `setup-dotnet` in CI, a new `global.json` pinning the SDK band, and the `.claude` session-start hook — it installed `dotnet-sdk-8.0` and gated on a version starting `8.`, so left alone every Claude Code web session would boot with an SDK that cannot build the repo.\n\n## The three changes that weren't mechanical\n\n**System.CommandLine** — all 31 option declarations are rewritten, not just the six that failed to compile. `Option<T>` now takes a name followed by *aliases*, so `new Option<bool>(\"--axfr\", \"Enable zone transfer…\")` still builds but registers that whole sentence as an alias and leaves the option undescribed. It is the one break in this migration the compiler cannot see. `SetHandler` also capped at eight bound parameters, which had forced a second parse of `args` inside the handler; `SetAction` receives the `ParseResult`, so that redundant parse is gone.\n\n**Swashbuckle / Microsoft.OpenApi 2.x** — `OpenApiSecurityScheme.Reference` is gone and `AddSecurityRequirement` now takes a document factory. Getting this wrong is quiet: inlining a fresh scheme instead of referencing the registered `\"Bearer\"` definition compiles and still produces a valid document, but Swagger UI stops attaching the `Authorization` header and every \"Try it out\" returns 401. `AddEndpointsApiExplorer()` had to stay — Swashbuckle's generator is built on ApiExplorer, and removing it fails container validation at startup (caught by 69 failing tests before it reached this branch).\n\n**Obsolete cert loading** — `X509Certificate2(byte[])` is obsolete from .NET 9. The three SMTP call sites hold DER and take `X509CertificateLoader`; the BIMI one holds PEM text and takes `CreateFromPem`. Sending PEM to the loader would have compiled and then failed on every VMC.\n\n## Verification\n\nEverything below was run locally on SDK 10.0.110 with a real Redis on 6380.\n\n- **Build** — clean, **0 warnings / 0 errors**, same as the pre-upgrade baseline. The four `SYSLIB0057` and two `xUnit2031` warnings the upgrade surfaced are fixed, not suppressed.\n- **Tests** — **447 of 448 pass**, matching the baseline exactly. Zero Redis self-skips, so all 43 shared-cache tests genuinely exercised the 3.0.17 client.\n- **CLI parity** — `--help` diffed against the pre-upgrade binary: identical but for two cosmetics (System.CommandLine no longer prints `[default: True]` for a boolean flag, and orders `--version` after `--help`). All 36 option and alias spellings verified to still parse. A full run over `example.com` returns the same **35 pass / 4 warning across 87 checks**, and CI's `Duration │ …s` regex still matches the Spectre table it parses.\n- **OpenAPI** — generated document diffed against the pre-upgrade build: same 23 paths, same operations, same 4 schemas, same security schemes, global security still `[{\"Bearer\": []}]`. Only change is the emitted spec revision, 3.0.1 → 3.0.4.\n- **Web service** — boots, `/api/defaults` byte-identical (ignoring the build stamp), `/swagger` 200.\n- **Container** — no Docker daemon in this environment, so the image build itself is **unverified** and rests on CI. What was verified: `mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/{sdk,aspnet}:10.0` both exist, and the Dockerfile's exact `dotnet publish` line runs clean and still stamps provenance — a published build reports `1.0.0, commit 5f0802b…, branch …, PR #999`.\n\n## Known and out of scope\n\n- `DiskCacheTests.RetryErrors_KeepsSuccessfulEntries` fails in this sandbox on **both** net8.0 and net10.0 — it asserts a live plain-HTTP request to example.com succeeds, which the egress proxy blocks. Environmental, pre-existing, expected green in CI.\n- An unknown option (e.g. `--nonexistant`) is silently swallowed rather than rejected. Verified identical before and after, so it is pre-existing, not a regression — but worth a follow-up.\n- **Not done deliberately**: `USER $APP_UID` in the Dockerfile (changes deployment file-permission posture), xunit v3 (a separate migration), and the versioning gap — with no git tags and no `<Version>`, every image still reports `1.0.0` and `sha-<short>` remains the only identifying tag.\n- The OIDC and JWT-bearer round trips can't be exercised here; they need a smoke test against real Entra before this ships. The code already sets `MapInboundClaims = false` with explicit claim types, which is what insulates it from the IdentityModel 7→8 mapping changes.\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_016pbZnikthqk94Ai5MCCxbT)_",
